 { In this introductory section, we'll cover a few general guidelines to help you make better decisions regarding which outposts to target for maximum effect. An outpost is usually valuable for many of the same reasons as a weak square. These include it being central, advanced, easy to reach, close to the action, near the enemy king, cannot be easily defended, etc. There are other questions you can ask yourself too. Does occupying the outpost come with a threat against the opponent's position? Can my opponent easily defend the outpost with their pieces? Is the outpost controlled by more than one of my pawns, or could this happen in the future? In the case that I can bring several pieces to the outpost, would one of them be more effective than the other? We are not going to worry about recalling these questions consciously during our games, as this would be likely to leave us confused or overwhelmed. Instead, we'll be developing our intuition over the course of the examples covered in this chapter and, later, the puzzle set. With that said, let's dive into our first position below! } 2. { -KEY- } Nd3 $1 { White correctly judges that the f6 square is too time-consuming to reach. Besides, the only open file of the position (the c-file) lies on the queenside, so the knight may find itself out of the action if it moves to f6. } ( 2. Nf3 $2 { It would be a mistake to drive towards the f6 outpost, even if it is the most advanced square. The problem is that the plan is too slow, and Black is able to generate enough counterplay in time. } a5 $1 { The natural pawn break immediately forces White to react. } 3. bxa5 Nxa5 { Black's knight will land on the strong c4 outpost next. } 4. Rfb1 Nc4 5. Rxb5 Nxa3 $11 { White's knight has made no progress towards f6, while the exchange of pawns on the queenside leaves Black without any problems. } ) 
Rfc8 $6 { Black plays a natural, but imprecise move. } ( 2... a5 $1 { An added benefit of the knight sitting on d3 is that now the b4 pawn is better protected, buffering White from the impact of Black's ... a5 pawn break. Nevertheless, this is what Black should have played, when after: } 3. Rac1 Ne7 4. Rc5 $14 { White has some pressure, but with correct play Black should be able to hold since they have active possibilities with moves like ...axb4 or ...Nf5. } ) 
3. Nc5 $6 { White returns the favour with a mistake of their own! The move looks very natural, immediately occupying the powerful outpost on c5. White's mistake was failing to spot that the best piece to place on the outpost was a rook and not the knight. When evaluating an outpost, it is necessary to find both the right square and the right piece. } ( 3. Rac1 $1 a5 { Now the break is a bit too slow. } 4. Rc5 $16 { Black has no good reply against the threats of Rxb5 and Rfc1. } ) 
a5 $1 { Now the pawn break is effective. Black has already equalised. } 4. Rfb1 { White tries to defend with the king's rook, but now Black can immediately simplify the position. } ( 4. Rab1 axb4 5. axb4 $11 { Black's control of the a-file offers them more than enough counterplay. } ) 
 ( 4. bxa5 Rxa5 $11 { The a3 and b5 pawns will likely be exchanged soon, reaching a drawn endgame. } ) 
axb4 5. axb4 Rxa1 6. Rxa1 Nxb4 { Black is up material for now, but they cannot hang on to the b5 pawn. } 7. Rb1 Nc6 8. Rxb5 Rb8 $1 9. Rxb8+ Nxb8 $11 { The rooks come off, with a drawn endgame. This quick analysis highlights some important principles: 1. Advanced outposts (like f6) are not always more desirable. Other factors like the time it takes to reach it can make it a poor option. 2. An outpost can be strong if occupied by one piece (e.g. a rook) but ineffective when occupied by another (e.g. a knight). 3. Always take into account the opponent's counterplay. Here Black's plan, based on the ...a5 pawn break, was as strong as White's outpost strategy. 4. Outposts tend to lose in strength as material comes off the board. The final position is an example of this at play. } 
