 { Choose the best move between 1.Nc5 and 1.Nf6. } 1. { -KEY- } Nc5 $1 { Correct! This move, or 1.Nd6, is one of the two winning jumps in the position. The nice thing about the c5 square is it comes with an immediate threat against the Black bishop on a6. } ( 1. Nd6 $18 { A great square, more central and advanced than c5. From here White targets the f7 pawn, but on balance 1.Nc5 is a little better as it comes with a threat against the bishop on a6. } ) 
 ( 1. Nf6 $2 { is a big mistake. Here the knight would be removing itself from the heart of the action without bringing any threats against the Black position. Importantly, since the a3-f8 diagonal is fully open, White cannot follow up with 2.Ba3. } ) 
Bc8 2. Ba3 $1 { Developing and renewing the threat of Na6+. } Qe8 3. Qf4 $1 { Pressing f7, but mainly seeking to bring the queen into the action via c1. } Nd7 { The outposted piece is too strong to be left unchallenged. } 4. Ba4 $1 { White was prepared to meet Black's idea. More pieces flood the Black queenside, while the knight on d7 is now pinned. } Bxc5 { The only way to eliminate the knight is with the bishop, but now the dark squares collapse. } 5. Bxc5 Rxb1 { Black is forced into another unfavourable exchange since White was threatening to win material with Bc5-d6+. } 6. Rxb1 Bb7 7. Bd6+ Kc8 8. Qc1 $1 { The last of White's pieces joins the action. Black will soon be mated. } Qd8 9. Qb2 Ba6 10. Qb8+ Nxb8 11. Rxb8# { In this example, the c5 and d6 outposts were similarly powerful. All else being equal, the d6 outpost would be better as it is more central and advanced. In this case, however, the presence of Black's bishop on a6 swung the decision in favor of 1.Nc5. } 
