 { White has two outposts to choose from. Pick the strongest move between 1.Nd2 (to go to c4 next) or 1.Nd4 (to go to c6 next). } 1. { -KEY- } Nd4 $1 { Correct! Most of the time, the more advanced square will be the right target. Here, it is an easy decision for White since not only is c6 more advanced, but once the knight lands it will hit various targets in the Black position. } ( 1. Nd2 $2 { This move would spoil White's advantage. The c4 square is a lot less advanced than c6, and it takes the same time to reach. Additionally, from c4 White has no direct threats. } a5 2. Nc4 a4 $15 { If anyone stands better here, it's Black. White's c4 knight is offset by Black's outposted knight on c5, while in terms of the queenside pawns Black has the more aggressive setup. } ) 
Qb6 { Black is forced to react and prepare for the invasion of White's knight onto the c6 square. } 2. Nc6 $16 { Black's outposted knight on c5 is no match for White's. Not only is their knight less advanced, but it has no direct threats or good forward jumps. } Rfe8 { Defending the pawn. } 3. a3 $1 { White can continue to build up the pressure in many ways, but opening up the a-file makes sense. } bxa3 ( 3... a5 { also does not save Black: } 4. axb4 axb4 5. Nxe7 $1 Kf8 6. Rxa8 Rxa8 7. Nc6 $16 { White is up a clean pawn. } ) 
4. Rxa3 { The structure has changed in White's favor. Black's c5 knight is no longer on an outpost, while White is threatening to play 5.Qa1 to put pressure along the semi-open a-file. Note the terrible power of the knight on c6. It is keeping Black's e8 rook tied to the defense of e7, while also targeting the a7 pawn. It is even supporting a possible b4 push. } Ng4 { Black is hoping that some piece exchanges will reduce the pressure, but the position is hopeless. } 5. Bxg7 Kxg7 6. b4 Nd7 7. Qa1+ Ngf6 8. Ra6 ( 8. Rxa7 $2 { Cashing in too soon would let most of White's advantage slip. } Rxa7 9. Qxa7 Qxa7 10. Nxa7 Ra8 11. Nc6 Kf8 $11 { Black's compact pawn structure and well-placed pieces should allow them to hold the balance in the endgame, in spite of being a pawn down. } ) 
Qb5 9. e3 { White focuses on improving their pieces and will only convert their positional advantage into a material one at the right moment. } Kg8 10. Rc1 $18 ( { Overprotecting the knight on c6 to be able to meet } 10. Rc1 Nxd5 $2 { with } 11. Ra5 $1 { In this way Black remains paralysed. Outposted pieces on the sixth rank often spell doom for the opponent. On the kingside, they can lead to a fast mating attack. On the queenside, they will often lead to a collapse of the queenside. Let's show one more example of this now. } ) 

