 { As in the previous example, White has managed to establish a knight on c6. It is such a strong placement that Black is already lost. There are two main reasons why the knight overwhelms the opponent. The first is that Black's dark-squared bishop is passive and cannot challenge the knight. The second is that White has a pawn majority on the queenside, which can be easily advanced with the help of the knight. As in the previous example, the knight monitors a lot of critical squares. The threat of Ne7+ ties down Black's rook, while the control of the b4, a7 and b8 squares will help when advancing the pawns. } 1. { -KEY- } b5 { Marching the pawns is the most straightforward plan. } Qb7 2. Qa7 $1 { Forcing the exchange of queens to clear the path for the pawns. } Qxa7 ( { If } 2... Qc8 { then after } 3. b6 cxb6 4. axb6 $18 { Black has no way of stopping the pawn. } ) 
3. Nxa7 e4 { The only attempt to generate counterplay. Black hopes they can make use of ...e3 or ...Bd4 possibilities. } 4. Nc6 $1 { The cleanest path to win, taking away the d4 square from the bishop. } ( 4. b6 $4 { White still needs to be careful here, as the rushed advance of the pawn would leave them at risk of even losing the game! } cxb6 5. axb6 Bd4 $1 6. Rb1 Rb8 $19 { The tables have turned and Black is the one with a winning endgame. } ) 
axb5 5. cxb5 { The knight does an excellent job of supporting the promotion square on b8 as well as stopping the possibility of ...Bd4. } e3 { Black's hope is to bring the rook into the game via e3. It is necessary to try to attack the pawns from behind, since White's knight controls the b8 square. } 6. fxe3 ( 6. b6 $4 { would throw away all the advantage. } cxb6 7. axb6 e2 $1 8. Re1 Bc3 $1 9. Rxe2 Rxe2 10. b7 Rb2 11. b8=Q Rxb8 12. Nxb8 $11 { with a drawn endgame. } ) 
Rxe3 7. Rb1 { Supporting the pawn from behind. Black has no resources left. } Bc3 8. b6 cxb6 9. axb6 Re8 10. b7 $18 { White will queen on the next move, since the outposted knight stops Black from being able to play ...Rb8. As we have already seen, there is a strong link between outposts and attacking chances. In the next couple of examples we'll explore this connection further. } 
