 { Here both sides have an outposted piece, with White to move. Pause here and consider which side you feel stands better, before reading further. Here's a list of the key elements of the position: (1) White's knight pressures the base of Black's pawn chain on b7, but this pawn is well-protected and stable. (2) Black's bishop is much more centrally placed, and it connects well with Black's central pawns. (3) If Black can push ...f4 and then break with ...e3, they will activate their bishop at the same time as they attack. (4) Black's bishop covers the c4 and b3 squares, paralysing White's knight. The combination of the bishop paralysing White's knight, while at the same time coordinating well with Black's broader strategic goal of advancing the pawn majority makes the position very favorable for Black. } 1. { -KEY- } Rc1 { White admits that the a5 outpost has no real value and prepares to play 2.Nc4. Outposts get a lot of their value from being close to the heart of the action, which in this case is the centre & kingside. } ( 1. a4 $2 { White does not have time to try to use their knight to make progress on the queenside. } g5 2. Rb1 { Preparing to push b4-b5. } g4 { Black breaks first. } 3. hxg4 fxg4 4. b5 axb5 5. axb5 cxb5 6. Rxb5 g3 $1 7. fxg3 e3 $1 { Due to the mobility of Black's central pawns, a hidden aspect of Black's bishop strength is revealed. In our starting position it was mostly an anchoring piece, lending stability to Black's setup. Now, the bishop becomes a monster in attack. } 8. Rxe3 { White has to eliminate the pawn, but now they lose control of the f1 square. } Qf1 $1 9. Kh2 Rf2 $19 { Black's bishop plays a key role in the attack on the g2 square. } ) 
g5 2. Nc4 Qf6 $1 { A useful move, anticipating possible Nc4-e5 or Nc4-d6 knight jumps, as well as making room for the Black rook on c7 to join the attack via g7. } ( 2... Bxc4 $2 { is a bad idea, since Black's bishop must be kept in order for the kingside attack to succeed. } ) 
3. Ne5 { This is the better choice of the two outposts, since from here White is able to cover the f3 and g4 squares, giving them more defensive options. } ( 3. Ne3 $5 { Probably the best move was to avoid the outposts and hunt down Black's bishop. However, after } g4 $1 4. hxg4 f4 $1 5. Nxd5 cxd5 $17 { it's hard to believe that White will be able to defend their king along the g-file. } ) 
Rg7 $1 { Black's heavy pieces are arranged effectively against the knight. Chess players call this technique 'playing around a piece' and it can be an effective approach when a piece cannot be directly challenged. } 4. Qc3 h5 { Black has no need to rush the breakthrough since White lacks an active plan. } 5. a4 { A last-ditch effort to strike up play on the queenside. } ( 5. Kf1 { Maybe the best chance was to try flee with the king. However, after } g4 6. Ke2 e3 $1 7. Qxe3 Bxg2 8. hxg4 fxg4 $19 { White is stuck between a rock and a hard place. The king wants to run to the queenside, but doing so will cost White the vital f2 pawn. } ) 
f4 6. Kf1 { A late attempt to escape. } g4 7. hxg4 ( { Black also crashes through after } 7. Ke2 { , for example } f3 8. gxf3 exf3 9. Kd2 g3 $1 10. fxg3 Rxg3 $19 ) 
f3 $1 { Shutting the king's escape route. } 8. gxf3 exf3 9. Re3 ( 9. gxh5 { allows a fast checkmate, the kind that you will have seen many times before. } Rg1 $1 10. Kxg1 Qg5 { The White king will be mated on g2. } ) 
hxg4 10. Ke1 g3 { Black's initial 4 vs 3 pawn majority is about to be converted to a deadly passed pawn. } 11. Kd2 ( 11. fxg3 { Taking the pawn leaves too many lines of attack open. For example: } Rxg3 12. Kd2 Rg2 13. Kd3 Qf5 { with mate next move. } ) 
g2 $19 { Black wins. This example higlighted a couple of general rules: (1) Central outposts are usually stronger than flank outposts. (2) An outposted piece gains in strength if it coordinates well with the rest of the forces. (3) An outposted piece can play a key supporting role in attack. (4) There is an important link between an outposted piece and the pawn structure. In this case the bishop combined well with Black's kingside pawns. Let's take a look at one more similar example. } 
