 { This position was reached in the game Botvinnik - Keres (1952). Can you guess what three-move sequence was played by White in order to first create an advanced outpost, and then immediately occupy it? } 22. { -KEY- } e5 $1 { The first step. White begins by carving out the d6 square as an outpost, as well as kicking the knight away from its defensive post on f6. } Nd5 23. Ne4 $1 { The second step. The White knight is en route to the outpost. } Nf8 24. Nd6 $1 { Correct! When evaluating the strength of the d6, we can note several factors: (1) The knight is both central and advanced, resulting in the control of many vital factors and undermining Black's coordination and defensive efforts. } ( { (2) The knight creates threats from the outpost, allowing White to speed up his initiative. If it were White's move, he could already play } 24. Nd6  25. Nxf7 { since after } Rxf7 26. Be6 $1 { White wins material. (3) The knight can only be challenged by Black's knights, but they cannot easily make contact with the d6 square. The knight's influence when combined with White's other assets (semi-open f-file, central control) is enough to already claim a winning position. } ) 
Qc7 25. Be4 { Opening up the possibility of Bxd5 at the right moment, as well as pivoting away from the outpost with Nf5. } Ne6 { Black's knights are quite central, but White can play around them. On the other hand, White's knight on d6 makes it difficult for Black to maneuver his heavy pieces. } 26. Qh4 $1 g6 { Protecting against the threat of Qxh7+, but now the dark squares become too weak. } 27. Bxd5 { Botvinnik continues his color complex strategy, eliminating the light-squared knight as this piece was controlling key dark squares like f6 and e7. } cxd5 28. Rc1 $1 { Grabbing the open file and forcing Black's queen to go even more passive. } Qd7 { Black has to move the queen here in order to keep defending the e7 rook. } 29. Rc3 { Adding to the attack. Note how Black's queen on d7 makes White's task easier, since it cuts out a possible exchange sacrifice on d6. } Rf8 30. Nf5 $3 { The centrally outposted knight quickly switches to the flank, breaking down the last of Black's defenses. } Rfe8 ( 30... Ree8 { Black cannot save the exchange. For example: } 31. Nh6 Kh8 ( { If } 31... Kg7 { then } 32. Qf6 Kxh6 33. Rh3 ) 
32. Qf6 Ng7 33. Nxf7 Kg8 { 33...Rxf7 would avoid checkmate, but the endgame is lost. } 34. Nh6 { White now mates in puzzle-like fashion. } Kh8 35. Qxf8 $1 Rxf8 36. Rxf8 ) 
 ( 30... gxf5 { Capturing the knight also loses after } 31. Rg3 Ng7 32. Qf6 $18 { With mate on g7 on the next move. } ) 
31. Nh6+ Kf8 ( 31... Kh8 32. Qf6 { would lead to the same outcome as in the variation after 30...Ree8. } ) 
32. Qf6 { White's attack is devastating, and only five moves later Black resigned. } Ng7 33. Rcf3 { Threatening checkmate by sacrificing the queen on f7! } Rc8 { Making space for the king. } 34. Nxf7 { The game is over only a few moves later: } Re6 35. Qg5 Nf5 36. Nh6 $1 Qg7 37. g4 $18 { Black resigned. This game highlighted the power of outposts that are both central and advanced, and the ability to use such outposts to conduct very powerful attacks against the enemy king. We also saw how, as in previous examples, the outposted piece on the sixth rank had a paralysing effect on the opponent. We'll explore this theme further with the final two examples of the section. } 
