 { We arrive at a position reached in game 2 of the 1908 World Championship match between Siegbert Tarrasch and Emanuel Lasker. Things are not going well for Tarrasch, since Black's bishop on e3 is heavily restricting the coordination and mobility of his heavy pieces. For now this square is 'only' a weak square, but with his next move Black will convert it into an outpost. } 29. { -KEY- } Rd1 f4 30. Nh1 d4 $1 { Adding a second pawn to the defense of the bishop, fully locking down the e3 square. The words of Peter Romanovsky, author of 'Soviet Middlegame Technique', come to mind here: 'If a bishop penetrates to the third (sixth) rank and establishes itself there - especially on e6/d6 or e3/d6 - this too is a significant and sometimes even decisive positional asset.' } 31. Nf2 $1 { Activating the knight and also setting up the possibility of bringing the king to h1. } Qa6 $6 { An inaccuracy, giving White one last chance to hold. } ( 31... Rg8 $1 { was stronger, as in this variation, White can no longer bring their king to h1. } 32. Kg1 $2 Reg5 $1 { The point. White is not in time to defend the g2 focal point. } 33. Kh1 Rxg2 34. Qxg2 Rxg2 35. Kxg2 Qg6 36. Kf1 f3 $19 ( { Or } 35... Qa6 35. Kf3 Qh5 37. Ng4 Qd5 ) 
 ) 
32. Nd3 $2 ( 32. Kg1 $1 { White missed the opportunity to reach safety on h1. } Qxa2 33. Kh1 Qa6 34. Nd3 $11 { Here White would have held the balance, as he could have 'played around' the Black pieces by organising himself along light squares. } ) 
Rg5 $1 { Tartakower, when discussing this game in his book '500 Master Games of Chess' remarks that 'As Black's heavy artillery has several files at its disposal, his pressure soon proves irresistible.' This freedom of movement of Black's rooks would not be possible without the outposted bishop. } 33. Ra1 Qh6 $1 { Black continues to exploit his piece mobility. The pawn on h2 cannot be defended. } 34. Ke1 { White tries to flee. } ( { If } 34. h3 { White will concede an outpost on g3, when after } Rg3 35. Qd5 f3 $19 { White is lost, since gxf3 is met by Rg1# } ) 
Qxh2 { Black restores material balance, and with all of his forces much stronger than White's, he is completely winning. It's worth seeing the game in full because of the very instructive final breakthrough on move 41. } 35. Kd1 { The sixth rank bishop is not just influencing the kingside. Even in the centre of the board, the White king is subject to its constricting effect. Black is winning, and Lasker converted the position in instructive fashion. The rest of the game is lightly annotated for those who are curious to see how things played out. } Qg1+ 36. Ne1 Rge5 37. Qc6 R5e6 38. Qxc7 R8e7 39. Qd8+ Kg7 40. a4 f3 $1 { By pushing the pawn, Black can now mobilize his bishop. } 41. gxf3 Bg5 $1 ( { The final blow is delivered by the outposted piece as it vacates the outpost at just the right moment. The pressure that Black had been building up along the e-file is released, with White's king caught in the crossfire. Black resigned, in view of } 41... Bg5 41. Rxe6 Rxe6 { when Black is threatening to capture White's queen as well as to give checkmate on e1. } ( { But not } 41... Bh6 $4 { since after } 42. Rg2 $1 Qxg2 43. Nxg2 $18 { White is the one that wins! } ) 
 ) 

