 { We'll finish the section with one more puzzle. Which move do you think is best, 1.Nc6 or 1.Na6? } 1. { -KEY- } Nc6 $1 { Correct! This move contains two threats: 2.Nxa7 and 2.Rxc5. The first would win material, but the second is equally strong, as it would damage Black's structure and leave White with a passed pawn. } ( 1. Na6 $2 { This move is tempting, since from here White targets the Black rook on c5. It turns out however that Black's defensive task is made easier with all the rooks off the board! } Rxc3 2. Rxc3 Rxc3 3. Qxc3 Qd8 $1 { Dropping back to keep the queenside under control. } 4. Qc6 { Optically, the position looks crushing for White as two of their pieces sit on advanced outposts. The problem is that outposts function like money. Their value is based on the ability to exchange them for something more tangible, like an attack against the king or an extra pawn. Here, White can occupy the outposts but not exchange them for anything else. } Nf6 5. Bf3 Be7 6. Qb7 Qd7 $11 { Black is in time to defend the a7 pawn, and so the position is completely equal. As we can see from this variation, it's important to avoid over-reliance on intuition, instead making sure to calculate concrete lines when possible. } ) 
Rxc3 { Black tries to avoid a bad change to their structure. However, they run into even bigger problems! } ( 1... Rc7 { Black can try to hang on to the pawn. } 2. Rxc5 $1 dxc5 ( 2... bxc5 3. a5 $18 ) 
3. f4 $1 { The c6 knight continues to be a monster, this time adding to the strength of the central pawn break. } exf4 ( { Black could try to avoid capturing with } 3... Bd6 { , but after } 4. Rf1 { they would be forced into playing } exf4 { anyway. } ) 
4. e5 Qf5 5. Qxf4 $1 { The cleanest move. Once the queens leave the board, White's central pawn mass will win the game. } Qxf4 6. gxf4 $18 { White's pawn advances will cost Black a minor piece at the least. } ) 
2. Rxc3 Rc7 { Black rushes to defend the pawn. We had said that this example involves the theme of 'paralysis'. We can see that the rook is tied up, the knight on e8 has no squares, and the bishop on f8 is passive. However, things are about to get much worse for Black... } 3. Rf3 $18 { White's pieces and pawns combine to trap the Black queen! This example showcased the interaction between positonal themes (space, outposts) and more dynamic elements like tactics and calculation. Developing your dynamic play will help you to make better positional decisions, and vice versa. This brings us to the end of the section. Hopefully you feel better equipped when choosing between outposts, as well as evaluating their strength based on different elements like piece coordination and the pawn structure. Next, we'll focus on two related topics: how to create outposts, and how to maneuver towards them. } 
 *
